\f1\b\fs22 The Confrontation: Jihad Man Revealed
\f2\b0 \
Gerry had been making his way through the chaos of the battlefield with the practiced ease of someone who had spent years positioning himself in exactly the right place at exactly the right time. His destination was a forward observation post that would give him optimal oversight of the battle while maintaining sufficient distance from anything that might explode, collapse, or spontaneously redefine the local laws of physics.\
What he had not anticipated was Special Agent Ajent Jones emerging from behind a cluster of ornamental bushes that had somehow survived the morning's festivities, straightening his black tie, looking like a man who had finally found the answer to a question that had been driving him slowly insane for months.\
"Gerry Hardman," Jones announced with the satisfaction of someone completing a particularly difficult crossword puzzle. "Or should I say... Jihad Man."\
Gerry paused, calculating distances to cover, escape routes, and the probability that Jones was operating under some form of psychological breakdown that might be leveraged to his advantage. "Agent Jones. I'm afraid I don't know what you're talking about."\
"Oh, but you do." Jones stepped closer, his service weapon drawn but not yet aimed, his eyes bright with the fever of absolute certainty. "You've been playing puppet master for years. Every crisis, every conspiracy, every mission that went sideways\'97you were there, pulling strings, making sure the chaos served your agenda."\
The accusation hung in the air between them like a particularly awkward dinner party conversation that had suddenly turned lethal.\

\f1\b The Challenge
\f2\b0 \
"You think I'm some kind of terrorist mastermind?" Gerry asked, genuinely curious about the psychological mechanics that had led Jones to this conclusion. "Agent Jones, I'm a mid-level bureaucrat with a talent for paperwork and committee meetings. Hardly the stuff of international conspiracies."\
"Mid-level bureaucrat who somehow rose through the ranks during every major crisis," Jones countered. "Mid-level bureaucrat who always knew exactly which investigations to pursue and which to quietly bury. Mid-level bureaucrat who managed to be present at every key moment in this entire Gyges Disc affair."\
Jones began circling Gerry with the predatory movement of someone who had finally cornered his prey. "I've been tracking the pattern for months. Every time something went wrong, every time an operation failed in exactly the right way to serve someone else's interests\'97there you were. The helpful administrator. The efficient problem-solver. The man who always had the perfect solution that somehow made everything worse."\
Gerry had to admit, when laid out like that, it did sound rather damning. "Correlation isn't causation, Agent Jones. Sometimes competent people simply rise to positions where they can be most useful during crises."\
"Useful to whom?" Jones snapped. "That's what I've been trying to figure out. Who are you really working for? What's the endgame? Is it about the Gyges Discs? The Elvi? Something bigger?"\

\f1\b The Argument
\f2\b0 \
"You want to know the truth?" Gerry said, recognizing that the situation had moved beyond the point where denial would be effective. "The truth is that incompetence creates opportunities for those willing to exploit them. Every agency, every department, every level of government is staffed by people who are more interested in covering their own failures than solving actual problems."\
He gestured toward the battlefield around them, where government forces were engaged in hand-to-hand combat with sequined warriors hurling reality-warping frisbees. "Look at this mess. Look at what we've become. A government so divorced from reality that we're fighting a war against Elvis impersonators using weapons that occasionally cause small earthquakes."\
"So you decided to take control," Jones said, his weapon now aimed directly at Gerry's chest. "You decided you knew better than everyone else how things should be run."\
"I decided to survive," Gerry corrected. "And occasionally, when the opportunity presented itself, to guide things in directions that might actually serve the public interest rather than the egos of incompetent politicians and paranoid administrators."\
Jones laughed, a sound with no humor in it. "The public interest? You've been manipulating events that led to thousands of deaths. You've helped orchestrate a war against civilians. You've enabled the deployment of biological weapons against an entire population."\
"And without my involvement, those things would have happened anyway, but less efficiently and with far worse outcomes," Gerry replied. "Sometimes the choice isn't between good and evil, Agent Jones. Sometimes it's between terrible and catastrophic, and you choose terrible because it's what you can live with."\

\f1\b The Fight
\f2\b0 \
The philosophical debate ended when Jones pulled the trigger, and Gerry dove behind a decorative fountain that had been designed by someone with strong opinions about the relationship between water pressure and artistic expression.\
What followed was a protracted engagement that combined the tactical complexity of urban warfare with the personal animosity of a particularly bitter divorce proceeding. Jones had superior firepower and official authority; Gerry had intimate knowledge of the terrain and years of experience in bureaucratic knife-fighting that translated surprisingly well to actual combat.\
They stalked each other through the landscaped gardens of Sublime Point, using ornamental shrubs as cover and decorative sculptures as improvised weapons. Jones maintained a steady stream of accusations about Gerry's various betrayals and manipulations, while Gerry provided detailed critiques of Jones's investigative methodology and psychological stability.\
"You never understood the bigger picture," Gerry called out from behind a statue of what appeared to be a very muscular cherub. "You were so focused on conspiracy theories that you missed the actual conspiracy happening right in front of you."\
"Which was what, exactly?" Jones fired three shots that chipped marble wings and caused the cherub to develop what looked like a distinctly worried expression.\
"That there never was a master plan," Gerry replied, moving to a new position behind a fountain that featured dolphins engaged in what might have been either playful frolicking or synchronized swimming. "Just a collection of incompetent people making increasingly desperate decisions, and a few of us trying to minimize the damage."\
The fight continued for twenty-three minutes, during which they worked their way through most of Sublime Point's more decorative outdoor spaces. Jones demonstrated the kind of relentless pursuit that had made him effective at his job, while Gerry showed the survival instincts that had kept him alive and advancing through years of government service.\
The engagement finally ended when a stray Gyges Disc, hurled by an enthusiastic Elvi warrior, landed between them and created a localized reality fluctuation that temporarily reversed the direction of gravity in a fifteen-foot radius. Both men found themselves hanging upside down in mid-air, staring at each other with expressions of mutual annoyance.\
"This conversation isn't over," Jones said as they waited for physics to remember how it was supposed to work.\
"Agent Jones," Gerry replied wearily, "given the current circumstances, I'm not entirely certain any conversation will ever be over again."\
When gravity reasserted itself and they both crashed back to earth, they discovered that their private war had become somewhat irrelevant. The larger battle was reaching a crescendo that suggested the universe itself was about to have some very strong opinions about how things should proceed.
